			<div class="content" id="post-content-4857113">
									

<p>Hi author!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Here's&nbsp;the&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">here&nbsp;it&nbsp;be</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Let me start off by saying that I do see the humor in how weird the basics of this concept are. The imagery of infectious hummingbirds is compelling.</p>
<p>However, I don't see this concept working as is. SCP articles are typically stories <em>disguised</em> as descriptive files. What you have here is a fairly straightforward &quot;Monster Manual&quot; entry, in which you present an idea for a creature and leave it at that. While that may have been a popular approach in the earlier days of the site, I think you'll find that things have progressed somewhat.</p>
<p>It's important to think about the story that your anomaly can tell, moreso even than the details of your anomaly's effects. I would recommend starting with a narrative you find interesting and then building an anomaly around it instead of vice versa. While you're at it, I'd also recommend checking out some Series VI skips just to get an idea of how story-driven most modern articles are.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Have a good day!</p>

